course: dictionary
lesson: 6346
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word mouthpiece has more than one meaning, so you need the context to choose the right one. Ask yourself what the sentence is about, then try each meaning in its place.
explain: «Clean the mouthpiece of your recorder before you play it.» Here the clues are recorder and clean. The mouthpiece is the part you blow into, not a person or a guard.
explain: «Rugby players wear a mouthpiece to protect their teeth.» Here the clue is protect their teeth. The mouthpiece is a soft guard worn in the mouth, also called a mouthguard.
explain: «The party's newspaper is the mouthpiece of its leader.» Here a newspaper speaks for a person, so mouthpiece means someone or something that speaks for another person.
fact: A mouthpiece is the part of a musical instrument, phone or other device that you put to or near your mouth.
fact: In sport, a mouthpiece is a soft guard worn in the mouth to protect the teeth; it is also called a mouthguard.
fact: A mouthpiece can also be a person who speaks for someone else.
fact: «Mouthpieces of trumpets and recorders are cleaned and not shared without cleaning, because germs pass between mouths.»
fact: «Brass players buzz their lips into the mouthpiece»; the trumpet, trombone, horn and tuba are brass.
exercise: «Clean your recorder mouthpiece before you play it.» In this sentence, what is a mouthpiece? | *The part you blow into | A tube that carries water | A soft guard for your teeth | A person who speaks for a group
exercise: «Rugby players wear a mouthpiece to protect their teeth.» What does mouthpiece mean here? | A kind of ball | A musical note | *A soft guard worn in the mouth | A part of a phone
exercise: «The party's newspaper is the mouthpiece of its leader.» What does mouthpiece mean here? | A guard for the teeth | A sort of drink | The part of a trumpet you blow into | *A person or thing that speaks for someone else
test: «Brass players buzz their lips into the mouthpiece; blowing without buzzing gives just a hiss.» What should a brass player do to make a proper sound? | *Buzz their lips into the mouthpiece | Blow without buzzing | Cover the window | Hiss loudly into the trumpet
test: Which word is another name for a soft guard worn in the mouth? | spokesperson | condensation | *mouthguard | trombone
exam: In «Water dripping from a recorder is condensation», what does the word mouthpiece mean in the gap under the mouthpiece? | A drop of water | *The part of an instrument you put to your mouth | A soft guard worn in sport | A person who speaks for others
